  "question": {
    "text": "<p>Number of complexes which show optical isomerism among the following is _________.</p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>cis</mo><mo>-</mo><msup><mfenced close=\"]\" open=\"[\"><mrow><mi>Cr</mi><mo>(</mo><mi>ox</mi><msub><mo>)</mo><mn>2</mn></msub><msub><mi>Cl</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></mfenced><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>-</mo></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mfenced close=\"]\" open=\"[\"><mrow><mi>Co</mi><mo>(</mo><mi>en</mi><msub><mo>)</mo><mn>3</mn></msub></mrow></mfenced><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>+</mo></mrow></msup></math><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mtext>, cis </mtext><mo>-</mo><msup><mfenced close=\"]\" open=\"[\"><mrow><mi>Pt</mi><mo>(</mo><mi>en</mi><msub><mo>)</mo><mn>2</mn></msub><msub><mi>Cl</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></mfenced><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>+</mo></mrow></msup><mtext>, cis </mtext><mo>-</mo><msup><mfenced close=\"]\" open=\"[\"><mrow><mi>Co</mi><mo>(</mo><mi>en</mi><msub><mo>)</mo><mn>2</mn></msub><msub><mi>Cl</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></mfenced><mo>+</mo></msup></math></p><p>​​​​​​​<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>,</mo><mo>trans</mo><mo>-</mo><msup><mfenced close=\"]\" open=\"[\"><mrow><mi>Pt</mi><mo>(</mo><mi>en</mi><msub><mo>)</mo><mn>2</mn></msub><msub><mi>Cl</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></mfenced><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>+</mo></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><mtext> trans </mtext><mo>-</mo><msup><mfenced close=\"]\" open=\"[\"><mrow><mi>Cr</mi><mo>(</mo><mi>ox</mi><msub><mo>)</mo><mn>2</mn></msub><msub><mi>Cl</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></mfenced><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>-</mo></mrow></msup></math></p>",
    "image": null
  },
  "options": [],
  "correctValue": "4",
  "solution": {
    "text": "<blockquote><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msup><mfenced close=\"]\" open=\"[\"><mrow><mo>Pt</mo><mo>(</mo><mi>en</mi><msub><mo>)</mo><mn>2</mn></msub><msub><mi>Cl</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></mfenced><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>+</mo></mrow></msup></math> is a square planar complex forms geometrical isomers (cis and trans). Trans isomer does not show optical isomerism since it is symmetrical, while cis isomer shows optical isomerism as it is unsymmetrical.</p><p>POS = plane of symmetry</p><p>COS = centre of symmetry</p><p>The compounds,  <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>cis</mi><mo>−</mo><mo>[</mo><mi>Co</mi><mo>(</mo><mi>en</mi><msub><mo>)</mo><mn>2</mn></msub><msub><mi>Cl</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><msup><mo>]</mo><mo>+</mo></msup><mo> </mo><mi>and</mi><mo> </mo><mo>[</mo><mi>Co</mi><mo>(</mo><mi>en</mi><msub><mo>)</mo><mn>3</mn></msub><msup><mo>]</mo><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>+</mo></mrow></msup></math> show optical isomerism as they do not possess any element of symmetry.</p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"></math><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>cis</mi><mo>-</mo><msup><mfenced close=\"]\" open=\"[\"><mrow><mi>Cr</mi><mo>(</mo><mi>ox</mi><msub><mo>)</mo><mn>2</mn></msub><msub><mi>Cl</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></mfenced><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>-</mo></mrow></msup><mo>→</mo></math>can show optical isomerism (no POS &amp; COS)</p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>trans</mi><mo>-</mo><msup><mfenced close=\"]\" open=\"[\"><mrow><mi>Pt</mi><mo>(</mo><mi>en</mi><msub><mo>)</mo><mn>2</mn></msub><msub><mi>Cl</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></mfenced><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>+</mo></mrow></msup><mo>→</mo></math>can't show optical isomerism (contain POS &amp; COS)</p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>trans</mi><mo>-</mo><msup><mfenced close=\"]\" open=\"[\"><mrow><mi>Cr</mi><mo>(</mo><mi>ox</mi><msub><mo>)</mo><mn>2</mn></msub><msub><mi>Cl</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></mfenced><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>-</mo></mrow></msup><mo>→</mo></math>can't show optical isomerism(contain POS &amp; COS)</p><p>Hence, among the given complexes <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>4</mn></math> complexes can show optical isomerism.</p></blockquote>",
